::ENG4_U04_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources show workshops help when students use realistic examples, receive feedback, and revise. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Workshops exist in schools.#Incorrect. This does not connect the source ideas.
=B. Transition workshops are strongest when they combine realistic tasks, feedback, and revision time.#Correct. This connects all three source ideas.
~C. Students should never revise.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source set.
~D. Examples are unrelated to workshops.#Incorrect. The source set says examples matter.
}
::ENG4_U04_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim best grows from the workshop sources?
{
=A. Schools should provide senior transition workshops that include realistic tasks and structured revision after feedback.#Correct. This claim is specific and evidence-based.
~B. Schools should cancel all senior support.#Incorrect. This ignores the evidence.
~C. Workshops are always perfect in every school.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
~D. Feedback does not matter.#Incorrect. Feedback is central in the source set.
}
::ENG4_U04_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: "marked drafts and an open door." Which analysis is strongest?
{
~A. The passage has papers on tables.#Incorrect. This summarizes details.
~B. The room is probably a classroom.#Incorrect. This is inference about setting, not analysis of function.
=C. The marked drafts and open door present revision as an ongoing process of preparation and growth.#Correct. This explains how details function.
~D. The projector is turned off.#Incorrect. This identifies a detail without explaining meaning.
}
::ENG4_U04_L08_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves the vague sentence "The sources are good"?
{
~A. The sources are good good good.#Incorrect. This is repetition.
~B. The sources are there.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
~C. The sources say things.#Incorrect. This lacks precision.
=D. The sources show that workshops are most useful when students practice realistic tasks and revise after feedback.#Correct. This revision is precise and evidence-based.
}
::ENG4_U04_L08_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which communication choice best fits a checkpoint response for an academic audience?
{
~A. Use slang and leave out evidence.#Incorrect. Academic communication requires clarity and evidence.
=B. State the point clearly, use evidence, and explain reasoning in a professional tone.#Correct. This fits the audience and purpose.
~C. Submit only a title.#Incorrect. This does not communicate the required thinking.
~D. Avoid explaining the answer.#Incorrect. Explanation is needed for mastery.
}
